  title: "Islamic Story for Kids About Generosity: The Red Toy Truck",
  seoTitle: "Islamic Story for Kids About Generosity | Noor",
  seoDescription: "Read a gentle Islamic story for kids about generosity — Ahmed saves for weeks for a red toy truck, then faces the hardest sharing choice of his life.",
  slug: "generosity-red-toy-truck",
  lesson: "Generosity",
  ages: "Ages 5–9",
  mins: "3 min read",
  summary: "Ahmed saves his coins for five weeks to buy a shiny red toy truck — then a new classmate's only toy breaks, and Ahmed must choose between his treasure and his heart.",
  moral: "Real generosity is giving what you love, not what you do not need. The gift that costs your heart a little is the one Allah loves most.",
  ref: "The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught that none of us truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself (reported in al-Bukhari and Muslim — meaning in our own words).",
  body: [
    "Generosity means giving what you love — not just what you do not need. It is easy to share the old toy you are tired of. It is much harder to give away the thing your heart wants most. This is the story of Ahmed, a shiny red toy truck, and the day he learned what generosity really feels like.",
    "Ahmed was seven, and every Friday his grandfather gave him two coins. Most boys spent their coins on sweets the same day. Ahmed did not. He had a plan. In the shop window on his street stood a shiny red toy truck with real rolling wheels, and Ahmed wanted it more than anything in the world. He kept his coins in a small tin box under his bed, and every night he shook the box and counted.",
    "After five long weeks, the box was finally full. On Saturday morning Ahmed ran to the shop, his tin box rattling in his pocket. The shopkeeper counted the coins twice, smiled, and handed him the red truck. Ahmed held it like treasure all the way home. He pushed it across the floor — vroom, vroom — and the wheels rolled perfectly.",
    "On Monday, a new boy sat in the corner of the playground. His name was Bilal, and his family had just moved to Ahmed's street. At break time, Bilal's old wooden car — the only toy he owned — broke in half when another boy stepped on it by accident. Bilal did not shout. He just picked up the two pieces and sat very quietly, and Ahmed saw his eyes get wet.",
    "Ahmed looked at his red truck. His heart whispered, \"Not this one. This one is MINE.\" He pushed the truck deep into his school bag. But all through maths class, the thought would not leave him: Bilal's quiet face. At lunch, Ahmed remembered something his mother always said: the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught us that none of us truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself. Ahmed loved the red truck more than anything. And Bilal had nothing.",
    "After school, Ahmed walked up to Bilal with the truck hidden behind his back. \"For you,\" he said, and pushed it into Bilal's hands. Bilal stared. \"But — this is yours. Your new truck.\" Ahmed nodded. \"It was. Now it is yours.\" Bilal's whole face changed, like the sun coming out from behind a cloud. He pushed the truck across the ground — vroom, vroom — and laughed the biggest laugh Ahmed had ever heard.",
    "That evening Ahmed told his mother everything, and waited for her to be upset about all that saved money. Instead she hugged him tight. \"My son,\" she said, \"today you learned the real meaning of giving. Anyone can give away what they do not want. You gave what you loved — and look how happy it made you.\" And it was true: Ahmed's tin box was empty, but his heart felt fuller than on the day he bought the truck.",
    "Lesson for today: This week, give away something you truly like — a toy, a book, or your favourite snack — to someone who has less. Notice how it feels inside. Real generosity is a little bit hard, and that is exactly why Allah loves it so much.",
    "Question: What is one thing you love that you could share or give this week — and who would be happiest to receive it?",
    "Source note: This story is original fiction. It is based on the Islamic teaching that none of us truly believes until we love for our brother what we love for ourselves (reported in al-Bukhari and Muslim — meaning in our own words).",
  ],
