  title: "Islamic Story for Kids About Honesty: The Lost Wallet",
  seoTitle: "Islamic Story for Kids About Honesty | Noor",
  seoDescription: "A gentle Islamic story for kids about honesty: Ali finds a heavy brown wallet on the school bus and faces the hardest honest choice of his life.",
  slug: "honesty-lost-wallet",
  lesson: "Honesty",
  ages: "Ages 5–9",
  mins: "3 min read",
  summary: "Ali finds a heavy brown wallet on his school bus, full of money and cards. Nobody saw him pick it up — and nobody would ever know if he kept it.",
  moral: "Give back what is not yours, even when nobody is watching. Honesty feels hard for a moment but gives the heart a clean, happy feeling that lasts.",
  ref: "Based on the Islamic teaching that the trustworthy person returns what belongs to others (meaning in our own words); story is original fiction.",
  body: [
    "Honesty means giving back what is not yours — even when nobody is watching and nobody would ever know. The Prophet Muhammad ÷ taught that there is no true faith for the one who cannot be trusted with what belongs to others (meaning in our own words). This is the story of Ali, a brown wallet on a school bus, and the hardest honest choice he ever made.",
    "Ali was eight, and every school day he sat in the same seat on Bus Number 4 — third row, by the window. One rainy Tuesday, he was the last boy on the bus, bouncing with excitement because school was over. He slid out of his seat and was about to run down the steps when something brown caught his eye. A wallet. A big brown leather wallet, fat and heavy, lying on the empty seat behind him.",
    "Ali picked it up. It was heavy with coins and folded notes. Inside there was an ID card with a man's photo, some cards, and a folded paper. Ali's heart started beating very fast. “So much money,” he thought. “I could buy the big football from the corner shop. I could buy sweets for a whole month. Nobody saw me pick it up. Nobody would ever know.”",
    "The bus driver saw Ali standing still. “Everything okay, champ?” Ali held the wallet tight. For a moment he felt like hiding it in his school bag. But then he remembered what his father always said at bedtime: “Ali, a Muslim returns what is not his — even if nobody is watching. Because Allah is watching, and He loves the honest one.”",
    "Ali walked to the driver and held out the wallet. “Sir, someone left this on the seat behind me. It is not mine.” The driver's eyebrows went up. He opened the wallet, looked at the ID card, and nodded slowly. “You are a fine boy, Ali. The owner will come to the school office to ask about this. I will keep it safe.”",
    "At home Ali told his mother everything — including the part about the football and the sweets. His mother hugged him and smiled. “You know what? You are richer than the money in that wallet, because you have a clean heart.” Ali felt the funny happy feeling inside that only honest choices give.",
    "The next morning, the head teacher came to Ali's classroom with a man in a brown coat. The man was smiling so widely his face hurt. “You are the boy who found my wallet!” he said. “My name is Mr. Farid. All my money, my cards, my license — you saved me a terrible day.” He gave Ali a small box of dates. “For your honesty,” he said. Ali shared the dates with the whole class that day, and they tasted better than any sweets.",
    "Lesson for today: If you find something that is not yours, tell a grown-up right away — a teacher, a parent, or the shop owner. Do not keep it, even for “later.” Honesty feels hard for a moment, but it gives your heart a clean, happy feeling that lasts much longer.",
    "Question: What would YOU do if you found money on the ground at school? Who is the first grown-up you would tell?",
    "Source note: This story is original fiction. It is based on the Islamic teaching about trustworthiness and returning what belongs to others (meaning in our own words).",
  ],
