  title: "Islamic Story for Kids About Not Wasting Food: The Clean Plate",
  seoTitle: "Islamic Story for Kids About Not Wasting Food | Noor",
  seoDescription: "A gentle Islamic story for kids about not wasting food: Hassan learns his grandfather's small rule — take a little, finish it all — and becomes a champion of the clean plate.",
  slug: "no-wasting-food-clean-plate",
  lesson: "Gratitude",
  ages: "Ages 5–9",
  mins: "3 min read",
  summary: "Hassan leaves half his rice on the plate every evening — until his mother and grandfather teach him a small rule that changes dinner time forever.",
  moral: "Take only what you can eat and finish your plate before taking more. Wasting food is like saying thank you with a frown; finish it with a smile instead.",
  ref: "Based on the Islamic teaching to eat and drink without wasting (meaning in our own words); story is original fiction.",
  body: [
    "Every grain of rice on your plate is a gift from Allah — and a gift should never be thrown away without care. This is the story of Hassan, a plate of rice, and the small habit that made his whole family smile.",
    "Hassan was seven, and he loved dinner time — but only the eating part. One evening, his mother served him a big mountain of rice with chicken. Hassan ate half of it, pushed his plate away, and said, “I am full!” Half the mountain was still sitting there, getting cold.",
    "His mother did not get angry. She sat beside him and said, “Hassan, do you know how many people worked for this rice? A farmer planted it in the mud. The sun and the rain helped it grow. A driver carried it to our shop. And I cooked it for you with love.” Hassan looked at the cold rice. “So many people,” he whispered.",
    "Then his grandfather, who was drinking his tea nearby, smiled and said, “In our house we have a small rule, Hassan. Take a little. Finish it all. Then take more if you want. That way, no food is ever left behind on the plate.” Hassan giggled. “Food cannot feel sad, Grandpa!” “Maybe not,” Grandpa winked, “but Allah sees how we treat His gifts.”",
    "The next evening, Hassan took only a small spoonful of rice. He ate every single grain. “Finished!” he announced proudly, and took a little more. He finished that too. His mother’s smile was so big that Hassan felt warm inside, like he had done something important.",
    "A week later, at school lunch, Hassan’s friend Danish left half his sandwich on the bench. “Take a little, finish it all,” Hassan said, copying his grandfather’s voice. Danish laughed — and ate his sandwich to the very last bite. Both boys felt like champions of the clean plate.",
    "Lesson for today: Take only what you can eat. Finish your plate before you take more. Food is a blessing from Allah, and wasting it is like saying thank you with a frown — finish your food with a smile instead.",
    "Question: The next time you feel full but your plate is not empty, what will YOU do? Who will you remember — the farmer, your mother, or your grandfather’s small rule?",
    "Source note: This story is original fiction, based on the Islamic teaching to eat and drink without wasting (meaning in our own words).",
  ],
  links: [
    { title: "Islamic Story for Kids About Sharing: The Two Red Apples", kind: "kids", idx: 11, href: "#kids" },
    { title: "Islamic Story About Kindness to Neighbours: The Extra Plate of Food", kind: "waqiat", idx: 6, href: "#waqiat" },
  ],
  showFullBody: true,
