  ref: "Amanat — guarding what is entrusted to you — is a sign of faith; the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was known as Al-Amin, the Trustworthy, even before prophethood.",
  body: [
    "In a small town, there lived a baker named Saleem whose bread was known in every street. People trusted his scales, his word, and his hands. One winter evening, his neighbour, an elderly widow named Fatimah, knocked on his door holding a small iron key. “Saleem,” she said, “I must travel to my sister's village for one month. This is the key to my house. Inside, in a wooden box, is the money I have saved for my daughter's wedding. I trust no one else. Will you keep this key safe until I return?” Saleem took the key with both hands. “It is an amanat,” he said. “It will stay safe, inshaAllah.”",
    "Two weeks later, hard days came to the town. The price of flour rose, rains spoiled the roads, and Saleem's shop earned almost nothing. One night he sat in his empty shop, worried about how he would feed his own family. Then his eyes fell on the iron key hanging by the door. A thought whispered: “Take a little from the box. No one will ever know. You can return it later.”",
    "Saleem stood up, held the key, and looked at it for a long time. Then he said aloud, to his own heart: “Fatimah did not trust my shop. She trusted my character. If I touch what is not mine, I may fill my stomach for one night, but I will starve my soul for years.” He hung the key back and went home hungry but peaceful.",
    "The next morning, a traveller passing through the town bought all the bread in Saleem's shop for the poor of the town, paying in full and in advance. Work returned. By the end of the month, Saleem's shop was busy again. When Fatimah returned, Saleem gave her the key and walked with her to her house. She opened the box, counted everything, and found every coin exactly as she had left it. Her eyes filled with tears. “People said you had a hard month,” she said. “You could have taken from this.” Saleem smiled. “The hard month passed,” he said. “But a broken trust does not come back so easily. Bread can be baked again. A good name, once lost, is much harder to rebuild.”",
    "Lesson from this story: Guarding a trust is not only about money. It is about being the same honest person in private that people believe you are in public. When a hard time comes, the honest person protects his character first — and Allah opens doors that worry could never open.",
    "Daily-life application: Whatever is left with you — a friend's secret, borrowed money, a task at work, or your family's trust — treat it as an amanat. Return it on time, keep it safe, and never use it without permission.",
    "Discussion question: Why do you think people trusted Saleem with their most precious things — his skill, or his character?",
  ],
  links: [
    { title: "The Shopkeeper Who Would Not Lie", kind: "waqiat", idx: 0, href: "#waqiat" },
    { title: "Islamic Story for Kids About Gratitude: The Warm Jacket", kind: "kids", idx: 6, href: "#kids" },
  ],
  showFullBody: true,
