  title: "How Can I Stop Being Jealous of Others? An Islamic Guide to Overcoming Hasad",
  seoTitle: "How to Overcome Jealousy (Hasad) in Islam: A Practical Guide | Noor",
  seoDescription: "Struggling with envy of others' jobs, looks or lives? A practical Islamic guide to overcoming hasad — 6 steps to a calmer, cleaner heart.",
  slug: "stop-jealousy-overcome-hasad",
  topic: "Character & Heart",
  asked: "Practical guide · Published 21 October 2026",
  summary: "You asked: “I can't stop comparing myself to others — their jobs, their looks, their lives. It makes me bitter and unhappy. How do I stop being jealous?”",
  ref: "The Prophet ﷺ warned that envy eats away at good deeds like fire eats wood (reported in Abu Dawud — meaning in our own words); “do not envy one another” (Muslim — meaning in our own words); Surah al-Falaq 113:5 (meaning in our own words). This guide is original educational wording, not a copied fatwa.",
  body: [
    "You asked: “I can't stop comparing myself to others — their jobs, their looks, their lives. It makes me bitter and unhappy. How do I stop being jealous?”",
    "Noor's answer: What you are feeling has a name in Islam — hasad, envy: the painful wish that someone else's blessing would disappear. It is one of the few sins the Prophet ÷ warned destroys good deeds themselves: “Envy eats away at good deeds just as fire eats away at wood” (reported in Abu Dawud — meaning in our own words). The good news? Envy is not a life sentence. It is a sickness of the heart, and every sickness has a cure. Here is a practical plan.",
    "1. Name it honestly: hasad, not “just noticing.” The first step is honesty with yourself. When you see a colleague's promotion and feel a sting — not admiration, but a wish it would be taken away — that is hasad. Say to yourself: “This is envy, and it will burn my own deeds, not theirs.” Naming it takes away its disguise. Note: simply wishing for the SAME good thing for yourself — without wanting it taken from the other person — is not hasad at all. Scholars call that ghibtah (healthy aspiration), and it is perfectly fine. Wanting your own promotion: fine. Wanting theirs to collapse: hasad.",
    "2. Starve it at the source: shrink the comparison habit. Hasad lives on comparison, and comparison today lives in the scroll — curated feeds of other people's best moments. Reduce the feeding: unfollow accounts that consistently make you feel bitter (even good accounts), set a daily screen-time limit, and spend that hour on your own goals instead. Comparison is a game you can only lose; the person you compare yourself to is comparing themselves to someone else.",
    "3. Change the dua: ask for yourself, not against them. When envy flares, turn it into a dua for YOURSELF: “O Allah, bless me with good like this — and increase THEM in it too.” This one sentence does two things: it redirects your heart from resentment to hope, and — beautifully — the angels say “Ameen, and for you the same” when you pray good for another (reported in Muslim — meaning in our own words). Your envy becomes a doorway for your own blessing.",
    "4. Make dua FOR the envied person — yes, really. This is the strongest medicine. The next time someone's success stings, say: “O Allah, bless what You gave them and increase it.” The first times will feel forced. Keep going. You cannot keep praying for someone's increase while secretly wanting their decrease — the heart cannot hold both. Scholars describe this as the practical antidote the early Muslims used: generous dua kills envy at the root.",
    "5. Turn admiration into a plan, not a wound. Ask: what do I actually want — the RESULT, or just relief from my own dissatisfaction? If you envy a friend's knowledge, the cure is studying, not bitterness. If you envy a couple's happy marriage, the cure is working on your own relationships. Envy is often a badly-worded message from your soul: “this is what I wish my life had.” Listen to it, then act on it instead of resenting it.",
    "6. Protect yourself too — gently. Islam also recognises the other side: if YOU are being envied (a new job, a wedding, a baby), the Prophet ÷ taught seeking refuge with Allah from the evil of the envier (Surah al-Falaq, 113:5 — meaning in our own words). That means morning and evening duas, trusting Allah rather than obsessing over other people's eyes, and — wisely — not boasting. There is a difference between gratitude (thanking Allah for a blessing) and showing off (displaying it to provoke). Share your joy; don't perform it.",
    "Verified-source note: “Envy eats away at good deeds just as fire eats away at wood” (reported in Abu Dawud — meaning in our own words). “Do not envy one another, do not hate one another” is part of the Prophet's ﷺ brotherhood teachings (reported in Muslim — meaning in our own words). “And from the evil of the envier when he envies” (Surah al-Falaq, 113:5 — meaning in our own words). All wording above is original educational explanation, not copied text.",
    "A gentle note on scholarly differences: scholars agree hasad (wishing the blessing removed) is blameworthy, while ghibtah (wanting the same good without wishing harm to the other) is allowed — the exact boundaries in complex cases are discussed differently by different schools. The remedies in this guide (dua, cutting comparison, gratitude) are universally recommended; specific spiritual-remedy routines taught by some traditions differ in detail, and you may follow the scholars you trust.",
    "Disclaimer: This is general educational guidance, not a personal fatwa or medical advice. Persistent bitterness that affects your sleep, work or relationships may also need a counsellor — Islam honours seeking help. For personal cases, please consult a trusted local scholar.",
  ],
